/* English strings of notes/currents-fields, harvested by tools/i18n.cjs (do not edit by hand: harvest again) */
VDI18N.add('en', 'notes/currents-fields', [
"Magnetic Fields of Currents: teaching notes",
"Magnetic Fields of Currents",
"A long straight current makes a magnetic field that circles it (right hand rule), with B = μ₀I/({#}πr): twice as far, half the field.",
"Fields of several currents add as vectors. Parallel currents attract and opposite currents repel, with F/L = μ₀I₁I₂/({#}πd) on each wire, equal and opposite.",
"Ampère's law: ∮B·dl around any closed loop equals μ₀ times the current through it. B on the loop depends on every current nearby; the integral depends only on the enclosed ones.",
"A current loop has B = μ₀I/(2R) at its center. Many loops side by side make a solenoid with a nearly uniform field μ₀nI inside, weak outside, and about half that at the ends.",
"Students should know the force on a current, F = IL × B. The demo has two scenes: <strong>Straight wires</strong> (a cross section, currents into or out of the page) and <strong>Loop and solenoid</strong> (a plane through the axis). Line spacing is exact for straight wires but only rough in the loop scene, so read B from the probe there.",
"A <strong>3D</strong> button (next to Flat) shows the same field map as a sheet in space. With straight wires, the wires run through the sheet and faint copies of the field lines sit above and below it: every slice across long straight wires looks the same. In the loop scene, the sheet is the plane through the axis, the turns wrap around it (their near halves drawn faint), and faint copies of the lines turned {#}° and {#}° about the axis show that the field is the same in every plane through the axis. Drag to turn the view; wires, the probe and the loop are moved in the flat view. Open it directly with ?view=3d.",
"<strong>One wire.</strong> Counterclockwise circles for current out of the page. Drag the probe from {#} cm ({#} µT) to {#} cm: B halves. <strong>Reverse current</strong> turns the compasses round.",
"<strong>Two wires, parallel currents.</strong> Ask Q1 before you press it. The force arrows show attraction, {#} µN/m on each. Drag the probe to the midpoint, where B is zero.",
"<strong>Two wires, opposite currents.</strong> Now they repel, and the fields add between the wires. Select a wire and change its current with the slider: the two forces stay equal (Q3).",
"<strong>Ampère's law.</strong> The loop encloses {#} A out and {#} A in: ∮B·dl = μ₀({#} A) = {#} µT·m, computed by integrating B along the loop. Drag wire {#} around outside the loop: the colors along the loop change, the integral does not. Drag the loop over wire {#} and the integral jumps. Switch to <strong>Rectangle</strong> and <strong>Clockwise</strong>.",
"<strong>Current loop.</strong> B at the center is {#} µT. Drag the probe along the axis and compare with μ₀IR²/({#}(R² + z²)^({#}/{#})).",
"<strong>Solenoid.</strong> N = {#} over {#} cm: {#} µT at the center against the ideal μ₀nI = {#} µT, and {#} of the center value at an end. Shorten L with the grip or slider until it turns back into a loop.",
"<strong>Electromagnet on a supply.</strong> {#} turns over {#} cm at {#} A: put the iron core in and the center field rises {#} times, from {#} mT to {#} mT. Ask why the gain is not μr = {#}: the iron's own poles push back (the demagnetizing factor is {#} for this shape). Stretch the coil to {#} cm and the gain climbs to {#}. Switch the supply to AC and watch the compasses swing as the field reverses.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Predict: two long parallel wires carry current in the same direction. Do they attract, repel, or feel no force? Use the field of one wire and F = IL × B on the other to decide.",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> Two long parallel wires are {#} cm apart and each carries {#} A in the same direction. Find the magnetic field that one wire makes at the other, and the force per meter on each. Check with Two wires, parallel currents.",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> In Two wires, parallel currents, wire {#} (at x = −{#} cm) is left at {#} A and wire {#} (at x = +{#} cm) is raised to {#} A. Where on the line between them is B zero? Which wire now feels the larger force per meter, and how large is it? Check with the probe and the force readouts.",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> In the Ampère's law setup, dragging wire {#} near the loop (but outside it) clearly changes B along the loop, yet ∮B·dl stays at μ₀({#} A). Explain how both can be true. Does wire {#} contribute nothing to B on the loop?",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> In the Ampère's law setup, what is ∮B·dl (a) as set, (b) with the loop direction set to Clockwise, (c) after the loop is moved or resized so that it also encloses wire {#} (+{#} A)? Give numbers in µT·m.",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> The Solenoid preset has N = {#} turns, L = {#} cm, R = {#} cm and I = {#} A. Find the ideal long solenoid field μ₀nI. The demo's exact value at the center is about {#}% lower, and at an end it is about half. Explain both, and predict what happens to the {#}% difference if L is doubled.",
"<strong>Q7.</strong> Open <strong>Magnets and Oersted's compass</strong>. (a) With two bar magnets, predict what happens when magnet B is turned around, then check. Cut magnet B in half: how many poles does each half have? (b) Switch to Oersted's compass: a wire {#} cm above the needle carries {#} A toward the north, and Earth's horizontal field is {#} µT. Find the wire's field at the needle and the angle the needle turns. Which way does it turn? Turn on <strong>Predict first</strong>, set a current, read the angle off the compass and work out B_E.",
"<strong>\"Magnetic field lines point from the wire outward, like electric field lines from a charge.\"</strong> Around a straight current they are closed circles; they have no start or end.",
"<strong>\"Ampère's law says B on the loop comes only from the enclosed current.\"</strong> B everywhere comes from all the currents. Only the integral of B·dl around the closed loop is fixed by the enclosed current; outside currents add as much along the loop as they take away.",
"<strong>\"The wire with the larger current feels the larger force.\"</strong> Each wire feels F/L = μ₀I₁I₂/({#}πd): the forces are equal and opposite (Newton's third law).",
"<strong>\"Like currents repel, as like charges do.\"</strong> For currents it is the other way round: parallel currents attract.",
"Magnets and Oersted's compass (OpenStax {#}): each bar magnet is two point poles (Gilbert's model), so only field directions and force ratios are shown. Oersted's needle settles at tan θ = (μ₀I/{#}πh)/B_E for a long straight wire running north and south above it; Earth's horizontal field is a setting.",
"Straight wires: infinitely long, thin and straight, perpendicular to the page, in vacuum. Each gives B = μ₀I/({#}πr), and the field shown is the vector sum. Field lines are contours of the vector potential A_z at equal steps, so their spacing is exact. A real wire has a thickness, and inside it B falls to zero at the center; the thin wire model leaves that out.",
"The force on a wire is per meter of length and comes only from the other wires.",
"∮B·dl is integrated numerically along the loop (adaptive Gauss–Kronrod quadrature, tolerance {#}⁻¹⁰ of μ₀Σ|I|). It never uses Ampère's law, so the agreement with μ₀I_enc is a real check. Compass needles line up with B; their swing is for show.",
"Loop and solenoid: each turn is an exact circle, coaxial and evenly spaced; its field comes from the full 3D Biot–Savart law in closed form (complete elliptic integrals). A real winding is a helix that also carries I once along the coil; that part is left out. The dashed gold curve on the axis plot is the continuous current sheet, B = μ₀nI(cos θ₁ − cos θ₂)/{#}.",
"History: the force between two parallel currents was the basis of the SI ampere from {#} until the {#} redefinition, which fixed the elementary charge instead."
]);
